WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:06.030
Figure fourteen. The software factory.

00:07.800 --> 00:30.004
The board from figure thirteen says one bead can start and ten wait. Something has to work it the way a team would: take what's ready, build it, have someone else read it before it lands, and pick up whatever that unblocked. In Keelson that's a swarm: a lead agent that owns the outcome, workers it spawns as the work calls for them, and a channel they all talk in.

00:30.804 --> 00:37.888
In the swarm launcher, paste the brief and the factory prompt, and pick Fleet: a lead and up to seven workers.

00:38.493 --> 00:43.435
Factory mode drops the turn and clock budgets. It runs as long as work keeps landing.

00:43.790 --> 00:48.844
Writers may change files, each in its own git worktree, and the lead may use the tracker.

00:50.824 --> 00:52.644
Start it.

00:54.324 --> 01:14.304
The factory prompt asks for three things one agent can't do to itself. Others critique the plan before anyone builds. A design critic judges every visible change from screenshots, and sends back one fix at a time. And no head merges until an agent other than its writer approves it. These are real lines from the channel.

01:15.204 --> 01:22.112
Twenty four minutes in, seven beads have shipped, and two writers build side by side as the graph widens.

01:22.604 --> 01:27.450
Then the board drains. Eleven of eleven, shipped.

01:28.404 --> 01:33.392
Forty six minutes, a hundred ninety turns, about twelve dollars at list price.

01:34.404 --> 01:37.792
On main, the writers' branches merged one by one.

01:41.704 --> 01:44.076
And a hundred twenty tests pass.

01:45.024 --> 01:55.708
And this is what they built. Cosmos, a planetarium at night. The filters live in the URL, and every object has its own illustration.

01:57.837 --> 02:00.113
Saturn, drawn as Saturn.

02:04.949 --> 02:07.321
A chill, counted on the server.

02:08.727 --> 02:22.019
A workflow runs one plan in one order, and that's right when you know the plan. A factory reads the graph, staffs itself, and checks its own work, so you get the review a team would give without being the bottleneck.

02:22.619 --> 02:25.311
Ready work in. Reviewed work out.

02:28.011 --> 02:30.873
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.
